tation-consistency","cwe":"CWE-1059","path":"README.md","line":32,"precondition":"A reader or downstream gate consults living docs for the current trust posture.","impact":"README.md:32 (committed bytes verified via git show) still says the workflow 'does not resolve the recorded daemon approval/capability trust-surface blockers or public signing/notarization prerequisites' — calling signing/notarization prerequisites and TS-001/TS-002 unresolved, contradicting the 2026-08-22 architect contract, CHANGELOG.md:40-44 and docs/release/SOURCE_INSTALL.md:6-11. docs/architecture/LOCAL_DESKTOP_MISSION.md:60 likewise still records the Approvals accept-condition integration as an unlanded 'recorded trust-surface blocker' although TS-001 shipped this epoch. SECURITY.md:62 scopes the unsigned/not-notarized non-claim to 'v0.1.x' only, leaving 0.2.0's identical posture unstated.","evidence":"README.md:32; docs/architecture/LOCAL_DESKTOP_MISSION.md:60; SECURITY.md:62; contradicted by CHANGELOG.md:9-19,40-44, docs/architecture/FORMAL_ASSURANCE.md:52-53, docs/release/SOURCE_INSTALL.md:6-27","remediation":"Update README.md:32 to state TS-001/TS-002 are resolved (linking config/formal-kernel-scope.json) and that signing/notarization are permanent non-claims per SOURCE_INSTALL.md; refresh LOCAL_DESKTOP_MISSION.md:60 to the resolved epoch; widen SECURITY.md:62 to cover all shipped 0.x artifacts. Leave archived receipts/mission contracts untouched.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"DCO-02","severity":"medium","category":"integrity-verification","cwe":"CWE-354","path":"scripts/verify_install_contract.py","line":195,"precondition":"The gate is run with a predecessor zip (always unverified) or with a successor path not actually listed in the adjacent SHA256SUMS.","impact":"The contract doc claims the gate proves 'hash verification' before anything else (SOURCE_INSTALL.md:96-104), but only a SHA256SUMS next to the successor is checked (:195-202); the predecessor is extracted and its binary executed (:214, :222) with no hash verification at all — the comment 'exact verified bytes' (:213) overclaims. Additionally `shasum -c` verifies whatever files SHA256SUMS lists in successor.parent; nothing binds arguments.successor's bytes to a SUMS entry, so a differently-named zip beside a valid SUMS passes the gate and is extracted and launched. The documented fail-closed hash gate is materially weaker than stated for both assets.","evidence":"scripts/verify_install_contract.py:195-202 (successor-dir SUMS only), :213-222 (predecessor extract+launch with no hash gate), :228 (successor extract not bound to a verified entry); docs/release/SOURCE_INSTALL.md:96-104 (gate claims)","remediation":"Require a SHA256SUMS (or explicit expected-digest argument) covering the predecessor and verify it before extract; after `shasum -c`, parse SHA256SUMS and require an entry whose name and recomputed digest match the exact successor (and predecessor) paths passed on the command line, failing closed otherwise.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"DCO-03","severity":"low","category":"fail-closed-enforcement","cwe":"CWE-561","path":"scripts/verify_install_contract.py","line":162,"precondition":"An asset argument is a symlink.","impact":"`predecessor = arguments.predecessor.resolve()` (:162-163) dereferences symlinks before the check at :164-167, so `asset.is_symlink()` can never be true; the documented symlink refusal is dead code and a symlinked asset is silently accepted (the resolved target is used). Consistency between the checked and used path is preserved, so residual risk is TOCTOU-shaped and low, but the stated fail-closed refusal is not enforced.","evidence":"scripts/verify_install_contract.py:162-167","remediation":"Evaluate `arguments.predecessor.is_symlink()` on the pre-resolution path (or use os.lstat) before resolving, and only then resolve for use.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"DCO-04","severity":"low","category":"unverified-receipt-claims","cwe":"CWE-345","path":"scripts/verify_ins